Comments:
Evaluated by Caleb Marshall 2024
1) All energies are from Basunia 2016 and are based on least square fits to the observed gamma transitions. See also Chipps 2016.
1) Direct capture was calculated from C2S values from 25Mg(d, p): Burlein 1984, Arciszewski 1984, and Hamill 2020.
2) All shell model calculations were taken from Richter 2011.
3) For the 163 keV resonance, all information was taken from Richter 2011. DWBA analysis of Hamill 2020 is inconclusive.
4) For the 377 keV resonance, the mirror C2S from Hamill 2020 was used.
4) For the 416 keV resonance, the proton partial width is a weighted average of Peplowski 2009, Temanson 2023, and mirror C2S.
5) For the 416 keV resonance, the gamma ray partial width is a weighted average between the value infered from Gg/Gp and Gp.
Gg/Gp is a weighted average of Perello 2022 and beta decay studies see below. Gp is a weighted average of the C2S from the
mirror (Burlein 1984, Arciszewski 1984, Hamill 2020) and 25Al(d,n) of Peplowski 2009 and Temanson 2023. Gg is then the
weighted average of the lifetime measurements of the mirror state (Ex=6125 keV 26Mg) (Glatz 1986, Canete 2021) with that deduced from
Gg/Gp * Gp.
6) The Gg/Gp ratio was evaluted from the beta decay gamma and proton intensities. The gamma intensities are averaged from
Perez-Loureiro 2016 and Liang 2020 and then converted using the branching ratio estimate of Wrede 2009,
while the proton intensities come from Thomas 2004 and Liang 2020 (see also Janiak 2017).
7) 782, 870, and 948 keV states were taken from the shell model. 870 and 948 keV states contradict assigned spin parities
are only used as a rough estimate for the transition to HF calculations.
8) Proposed level at 5668 keV by Canete 2023 was excluded since evidence of its spin parity and thus its mirror assignment is
weak. The evidence for its existence is stronger, but still tentative. The proposed mirror state at 5711 keV state in 26Mg
is most most likely negative parity based on Bhattacharjee 2014, Burlein 1984, and Arciszewski 1984. The l=1 assignment
in Canete 2021 is in tension with Burlein 1984 and Arciszewski 1984 who see evidence of a weak l=3 compenent of the unresolved doublet.